{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-132605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/799fd5d6528a9a66060d47e8f7613643ee5a305ed0dc494fabc1fc0ecf089e84.png)\n\nThe graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. The points $A,B,$ and $C$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x=-3,$ $x=0,$ and $x=3.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $A,B,$ and $C,$ find the intervals where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B.$ Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nThe information about ${f}^{′}(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,-3) & -3 & (-3,0) & 0 & (0,3) & 3 & (3,\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ & \\text{no extr.} & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ \\\\ {f}^{′} & + & 0 & - & 0 & - & 0 & +\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and $(3,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "459e3f05c236391edd9c7055cd83c3237fce91edab856e4b2d53085e190b86a0"} {"question" "q-132605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "v