en ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nThe information about ${f}^{′}(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,0) & 0 & (0,3) & 3 & (3,\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ \\\\ {f}^{′} & - & 0 & + & 0 & -\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ on the interval $(0,3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-73443" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "459e3f05c236391edd9c7055cd83c3237fce91edab856e4b2d53085e190b86a0"} {"question" "q-73443" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-73443" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-\\infty ,0)"] ["math" "(0,3)"] ["math" "(3,\\infty )"] ["text" "$(-\\infty ,0)$ and  $(3,\\infty )$"] ["text" "No interval exists where ${f}^{′}(x)>0$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-73443" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(0,3)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/3f542d08fede8a5bad978b62bece12070b02e0d3b4369c804454356f07d0832a.png)\n\nThe graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. The points $A$ and $B$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x=-3$ and $x=2.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at both $A$ and $B,$ find the intervals where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B.$ Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nThe information about ${f}^{′}(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,-3) & -3 & (-3,2) & 2 & (2,\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ & \\text{no extr.} & ↗ \\\\ {f}^{′} & - & 0 & + & 0 & +\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ on the intervals $(-3,2)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "459e3f05c236391edd9c7055cd83c3237fce91edab856e4b2d53085e190b86a0"} {"question" "q-132598" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132598" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(2,\\infty )"] ["text" "$(-3,-1)$ and $(2,\\infty )$"] ["text" "$(-3,0)$ and $(3,\\infty )$"] ["text" "$(-3,0)$ and $(4,\\infty )$"] ["text" "$(-3,2)$ and $(2,\\infty )$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132598" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(-3,2)$ and $(2,\\infty )$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-343536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $g(x)=3{x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+1$ is defined for $x\\in (-8,8),$ find the values of $x$ for which the function is strictly decreasing.\n\n$x\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-343536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function is strictly decreasing where ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =12{x}^{3}-24{x}^{2}+12x \\\\  & =12x({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\  & =12x{(x-1)}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n12x{(x-1)}^{2}<0.\n$$\n\nSince $(x-1)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all real $x$, the sign of $12x{(x-1)}^{2}$ is determined by the sign of $12x$, so $12x{(x-1)}^{2}<0$ exactly when $12x<0.$ This yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x & <0 \\\\ x & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is restricted to $x\\in (-8,8),$ we conclude that $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval $x\\in (-8,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-343536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "459e3f05c236391edd9c7055cd83c3237fce91edab856e4b2d53085e190b86a0"} {"question" "q-343536" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-343536" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-343536" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-343536" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132666" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)={e}^{2{x}^{2}-6x+3}$ is strictly decreasing." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132666" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function is strictly decreasing where ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)={e}^{2{x}^{2}-6x+3}(4x-6),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2{x}^{2}-6x+3}(4x-6)<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe exponential function is always positive, so this reduces to\n\n$$\n4x-6<0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-6 & <0 \\\\ 4x & <6 \\\\ x & <\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly decreasing on the interval $(-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132666" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "459e3f05c236391edd9c7055cd83c3237fce91edab856e4b2d53085e190b86a0"} {"question" "q-132666" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132666" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\in (-3,\\infty )"] ["math" "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)"] ["math" "x\\in (-\\infty ,3)"] ["math" "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2})"] ["math" "x\\in (\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132666" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-27{x}^{2}-30x+9$ is strictly decreasing." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function is strictly decreasing where ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =12{x}^{2}-54x-30 \\\\  & =6(2{x}^{2}-9x-5),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n6(2{x}^{2}-9x-5)<0.\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n6(2x+1)(x-5)<0,\n$$\n\nand the graph of $y=6(2x+1)(x-5)$ has roots at $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=5,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/7eec3fc84b851b3b39e5ae8ecff1343f39393d1dd8f7a6cce8e8904dc1c65d48.png)\n\nThe graph is negative on the interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},5)$ between the roots, so we conclude that ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ on the interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},5).$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},5).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "459e3f05c236391edd9c7055cd83c3237fce91edab856e4b2d53085e190b86a0"} {"question" "q-132671" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132671" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\in (-5,5)"] ["math" "x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},5)"] ["math" "x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )"] ["math" "x\\in (-\\infty ,5)"] ["math" "x\\in (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132671" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},5)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}+1)$ is strictly decreasing." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function is strictly decreasing where ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot 4x \\\\  & =\\frac{4x}{2{x}^{2}+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x}{2{x}^{2}+1}<0.\n$$\n\nThe function $2{x}^{2}+1$ is always positive. So, this reduces to\n\n$$\n4x<0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & <0 \\\\ x & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval $(-\\infty ,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "459e3f05c236391edd9c7055cd83c3237fce91edab856e4b2d53085e190b86a0"} {"question" "q-132667" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132667" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{2})"] ["math" "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)"] ["math" "x\\in (-\\infty ,1)"] ["math" "x\\in (-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})"] ["math" "x\\in (\\sqrt{2},\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132667" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-21017" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)=5-7x-4{x}^{2}$ is strictly decreasing." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-21017" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function is strictly decreasing where ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-7-8x \\\\  & =-8(x+\\frac{7}{8}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n-8(x+\\frac{7}{8})<0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8(x+\\frac{7}{8}) & <0 \\\\ 0 & <8(x+\\frac{7}{8}) \\\\ 0 & <x+\\frac{7}{8} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{8} & <x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval $x\\in (-\\frac{7}{8},\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-21017" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "459e3f05c236391edd9c7055cd83c3237fce91edab856e4b2d53085e190b86a0"} {"question" "q-21017" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-21017" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\in (-\\frac{7}{8},\\infty )"] ["math" "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{8})"] ["math" "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{7}{8})"] ["math" "x\\in (0,\\infty )"] ["math" "x\\in (\\frac{7}{8},\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-21017" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\in (-\\frac{7}{8},\\infty )"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-344091" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $g(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x+1$ is strictly increasing.\n\n$x<$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,x>$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-344091" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function is strictly increasing where ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={x}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2}>0.\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{1}{2})(x-3)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=(x-\\frac{1}{2})(x-3)$ has roots at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=3,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/26374f7e5c86c5585d384b3e65b4a1b3fb2f94172ed753cdb3fe7bdb0af0abb8.png)\n\nFor an upward-opening parabola, the product $(x-\\frac{1}{2})(x-3)$ is positive when $x<\\frac{1}{2}$ or $x>3.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the intervals \n\n$$\nx<\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{and}\\,x>3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-344091" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "459e3f05c236391edd9c7055cd83c3237fce91edab856e4b2d53085e190b86a0"} {"question" "q-344091" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-344091" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-344091" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-344091" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-48860" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/61b89de34a6900d2ea39e5c2af141b3353e9ca790ac2748d0fc6d4d578127f93.png)\n\nFind the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ (plotted above) is strictly increasing." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-48860" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function is strictly increasing where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $f(x)$ using the product  rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1>0.\n$$\n\nSolving $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=-1$ gives the critical point\n\n$$\nx={e}^{-1}\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{e}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that this critical point is a minimum. We also see that the slope of the tangent lines is negative to the left of the critical point and positive to the right of the critical point. \n\nSo, we summarize the sign of the derivative in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\begin{aligned}x & (0,\\frac{1}{e}) & \\frac{1}{e} & (\\frac{1}{e},\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ \\\\ {f}^{′} & - & 0 & +\\end{aligned}\\right|\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the interval $(\\frac{1}{e},\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-48860" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "459e3f05c236391edd9c7055cd83c3237fce91edab856e4b2d53085e190b86a0"} {"question" "q-48860" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-48860" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\in (0,\\frac{1}{e})"] ["math" "x\\in (0,e)"] ["math" "x\\in (\\frac{1}{e},\\infty )"] ["math" "x\\in (\\frac{1}{e},e)"] ["math" "x\\in (e,\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-48860" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\in (\\frac{1}{e},\\infty )"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-21019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $f(x)={x}^{3}+\\frac{3}{x}$ is defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ find the values of $x$ for which the function is strictly increasing." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-21019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function is strictly increasing where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}>0.\n$$\n\nUsing that $x>0,$ the above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) & >0 \\\\ {x}^{4}-1 & >0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)(x+1)(x-1) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factors ${x}^{2}+1$ and $x+1$ are positive, so this reduces to\n\n$$\nx-1>0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & >0 \\\\ x & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ is strictly increasing on $(1,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-21019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "459e3f05c236391edd9c7055cd83c3237fce91edab856e4b2d53085e190b86a0"} {"question" "q-21019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-21019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\in (0,1)"] ["math" "x\\in (0,3)"] ["math" "x\\in (0,\\infty )"] ["math" "x\\in (1,\\infty )"] ["math" "x\\in (3,\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-21019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\in (1,\\infty )"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-344093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $g(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}$ is strictly increasing.\n\n$x>$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-344093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function is strictly increasing where ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={x}^{3}-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x-4),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(x-4)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y={x}^{2}(x-4)$ has roots at $x=0$ and $x=4,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/bfba80e21f4a458af6c4111191908a9d45d7d8c05f237db293cdad71a451d943.png)\n\nSince ${x}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all real $x,$ the product ${x}^{2}(x-4)$ is positive exactly when $x-4>0,$ that is, when $x>4.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the interval \n\n$$\nx>4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-344093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "459e3f05c236391edd9c7055cd83c3237fce91edab856e4b2d53085e190b86a0"} {"question" "q-344093" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-344093" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-48855" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})$ is defined for $x\\in (-2\\pi ,0),$ find the values of $x$ for which the function is strictly increasing." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-48855" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function is strictly increasing where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain is $x\\in (-2\\pi ,0),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\pi  & <x<0 \\\\ -\\pi  & <\\frac{x}{2}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that cosine is positive in the first and the fourth quadrant. So, this reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} & <\\frac{x}{2}<0 \\\\ -\\pi  & <x<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)$ is strictly increasing on the interval $-\\pi <x<0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-48855" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "459e3f05c236391edd9c7055cd83c3237fce91edab856e4b2d53085e190b86a0"} {"question" "q-48855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-48855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\pi <x<\\pi "] ["math" "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<0"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}<x<0"] ["math" "-\\pi <x<-\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "-\\pi <x<0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-48855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\pi <x<0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-73465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/c4f2a9119ff41c5f9b34575c26f3df626df9f2db6d55bb3bc4f5709598055c82.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. Which of the following statements is true?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-73465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the graph, we see that\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is zero, so ${f}^{′}(2)=0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=3$ is negative, so ${f}^{′}(3)<0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=6$ is positive, so ${f}^{′}(6)>0.$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(3)<{f}^{′}(2)<{f}^{′}(6).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-73465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "459e3f05c236391edd9c7055cd83c3237fce91edab856e4b2d53085e190b86a0"} {"question" "q-73465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-73465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{f}^{′}(2)<{f}^{′}(3)<{f}^{′}(6)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(2)<{f}^{′}(6)<{f}^{′}(3)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(3)<{f}^{′}(2)<{f}^{′}(6)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(3)<{f}^{′}(6)<{f}^{′}(2)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(6)<{f}^{′}(2)<{f}^{′}(3)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-73465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{f}^{′}(3)<{f}^{′}(2)<{f}^{′}(6)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/e0bd848039c3f2db6dd298af47eb907035c47c0c22c1cc72afaaa233f29a0195.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. Which of the following statements is true?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the graph, we see that\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is negative, so ${f}^{′}(-1)<0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is zero, so ${f}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is positive, so ${f}^{′}(2)>0.$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(0)<{f}^{′}(2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "459e3f05c236391edd9c7055cd83c3237fce91edab856e4b2d53085e190b86a0"} {"question" "q-132691" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132691" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(0)<{f}^{′}(2)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(2)<{f}^{′}(0)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(0)<{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(2)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(0)<{f}^{′}(2)<{f}^{′}(-1)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(2)<{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(0)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132691" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(0)<{f}^{′}(2)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132682" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/0ba11dcbffa05bb78ee8d9bdd6ca8618c789cf13ab208f17d169a7f700cf9e3e.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. Which of the following statements is true?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132682" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the graph, we see that\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-2$ is zero, so ${f}^{′}(-2)=0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is positive, so ${f}^{′}(2)>0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=5$ is negative, so ${f}^{′}(5)<0.$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(5)<{f}^{′}(-2)<{f}^{′}(2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132682" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "459e3f05c236391edd9c7055cd83c3237fce91edab856e4b2d53085e190b86a0"} {"question" "q-132682" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132682" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{f}^{′}(-2)<{f}^{′}(2)<{f}^{′}(5)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(-2)<{f}^{′}(5)<{f}^{′}(2)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(2)<{f}^{′}(5)<{f}^{′}(-2)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(5)<{f}^{′}(-2)<{f}^{′}(2)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(5)<{f}^{′}(2)<{f}^{′}(-2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-132682" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{f}^{′}(5)<{f}^{′}(-2)<{f}^{′}(2)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-49006" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/ae52c0cfa5238c68574fdc3eff16618b84ce2dfaa5846df5300bd5602b8628f5.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following statements is true?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-49006" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the graph, we see that\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-2$ is negative, so ${f}^{′}(-2)<0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is positive, so ${f}^{′}(-1)>0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is zero, so ${f}^{′}(1)=0.$\n\nTherefore , ${f}^{′}(-2)<{f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(-1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-49006" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "459e3f05c236391edd9c7055cd83c3237fce91edab856e4b2d53085e190b86a0"} {"question" "q-49006" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-49006" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(-2)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(-2)<{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(1)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(-2)<{f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(-1)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(-2)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(-2)<{f}^{′}(-1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-49006" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{f}^{′}(-2)<{f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(-1)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-48996" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/0a55a722c0b1f22b7a3d4c1f8d69869aa6a7dd571f85349049389c105b7190aa.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. Which of the following statements is true?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-48996" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the graph, we see that\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is positive, so ${f}^{′}(0)>0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is negative, so ${f}^{′}(1)<0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=3$ is zero, so ${f}^{′}(3)=0.$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(3)<{f}^{′}(0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-48996" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "459e3f05c236391edd9c7055cd83c3237fce91edab856e4b2d53085e190b86a0"} {"question" "q-48996" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-48996" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{f}^{′}(0)<{f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(3)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(0)<{f}^{′}(3)<{f}^{′}(1)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(0)<{f}^{′}(3)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(3)<{f}^{′}(0)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(3)<{f}^{′}(0)<{f}^{′}(1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "q-48996" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(3)<{f}^{′}(0)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/state.json" "file_sha256" "063712de5aec34643d50968e6bdee7f1fa61ab590844c7c8b87cf01c3ddf9379"} {"question" "e-3722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ is shown below. The points $A$ and $B$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x=1$ and $x=4.$ Given that $f'(x) = 0$ at both $A$ and $B,$ find the intervals where $f'(x)>0.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/1a8208a75b7df62a0c692e08f74509ecf1462e3ab1f8dd7eae85a56272abde48.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/example-3722.json" "file_sha256" "03f8665f6a13e4302234ab06591845e561167d31b66e1525fe00c0e55f8a2a5e"} {"question" "e-3722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We have $f'(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B.$ Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then $f'(x)>0.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then $f'(x)<0.$\n\nThe information about $f'(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,1) & 1 & (1,4) & 4 & (4,+\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ \\\\ {f}^{′} & - & 0 & + & 0 & -\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f'(x)>0$ on the interval $(1,4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/example-3722.json" "file_sha256" "03f8665f6a13e4302234ab06591845e561167d31b66e1525fe00c0e55f8a2a5e"} {"question" "e-3728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x) = x^3 + x^2 - 8x$ is strictly decreasing." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/example-3728.json" "file_sha256" "75703c2e23ca686df31684d0fd6f3135601e993a18b206e69815bc26d252f474"} {"question" "e-3728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find where $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, we need to solve the inequality $f'(x) < 0.$\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+2x-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+2x-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n3(x + 2)\\left(x -\\dfrac 4 3\\right) < 0,\n$$\n\nand the graph of $y=3(x+2)\\left(x -\\dfrac 4 3\\right)$ has roots at $x=-2$ and $x=\\dfrac 4 3,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/14e4f6078b21d209082c548f7f5d064abeaa98ee737aff919d2f6efad7a317e8.png)\n\nThe graph is negative on the interval $\\left( -2, \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ between the roots, so we conclude that $f'(x) < 0$ on the interval $\\left( -2, \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval $\\left( -2, \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/example-3728.json" "file_sha256" "75703c2e23ca686df31684d0fd6f3135601e993a18b206e69815bc26d252f474"} {"question" "e-3731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{\\ln{2x}}{x}$ (plotted below) is strictly increasing.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/2ccb7c95d06c78a1730f9ca46aad9b6f1370050c745f2c1fc9208275670c3649.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/example-3731.json" "file_sha256" "b53a4f8e8bf46d4231caeead87ca42305259826fa45f856fdb78f2fd45133f20"} {"question" "e-3731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find where $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we need to solve the inequality $f'(x) > 0.$\n\nDifferentiating $f(x)$ using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\cdot 1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x}{{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1-\\ln{2x}}{x^2} > 0.\n$$\n\nThe denominator is  always positive, so this reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x>0\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x<1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $\\ln{2x} = 1$ gives the critical point\n\n$$\n2x = e\\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad x = \\dfrac{1}{2}e.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that this critical point is a maximum. We also see that the slope of the tangent lines is positive to the left of the critical point and negative to the right of the critical point. \n\nWe summarize this information in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\begin{aligned}x & (0,\\frac{1}{2}e) & \\frac{1}{2}e & (\\frac{1}{2}e,\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ \\\\ {f}^{′} & + & 0 & -\\end{aligned}\\right|\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the interval $\\left(0,\\frac 1 2 e\\right),$ which can also be written as $0 < x < \\dfrac 1 2 e.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/example-3731.json" "file_sha256" "b53a4f8e8bf46d4231caeead87ca42305259826fa45f856fdb78f2fd45133f20"} {"question" "e-3734" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/aa8ee7171576bc92bb9b6a6730223e2629bc0d6216cb54a7a8a9f51d97985d14.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements is true?\n\n- $f'(2.5) < f'(1) < f'(-1)$\n\n- $f'(-1) < f'(1) < f'(2.5)$\n\n- $f'(1) < f'(-1) < f'(2.5)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/example-3734.json" "file_sha256" "97594b2f253411de68b2465d2e015dfb22db8e5826151075d9dd411d2e591484"} {"question" "e-3734" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the graph, we see that\n\n- the slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is positive, so $f'(-1) > 0.$\n\n- the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is zero, so $f'(1) = 0.$\n\n- the slope of the tangent at $x=2.5$ is negative, and so $f'(2.5) < 0.$\n\nTherefore, only statement I is correct." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/example-3734.json" "file_sha256" "97594b2f253411de68b2465d2e015dfb22db8e5826151075d9dd411d2e591484"}] "usage" {"e-3731" [] "e-3728" [] "e-3722" [] "q-18227" [] "q-48996" [] "e-3734" []} "basis" 1190 "retractions" [[17592186106015 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1359/Source/Images/q-18227.png)\n\nThe graph of the continuous function $y = f(x)$ is shown below. The points $A$ and $B$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x = 0$ and $x = 2$. Given that $f^{′}(x) = 0$ at both $A$ and $B$, find the intervals where $f^{′}(x) < 0$."] [17592186106288 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1359/Source/Images/q-48996.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y = f(x)$ above. Which of the following statements is true?"] [17592186105489 :question/problem "The graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ is shown below. The points $A$ and $B$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x=1$ and $x=4$. Given that $f'(x) = 0$ at both $A$ and $B$, find the intervals where $f'(x)>0$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1359/Source/Images/188a9a9a3d8e58ae595eacd0c7a3e567.png)"] [17592186105489 :question/worked-solution "We have $f'(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B$. Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then $f'(x)>0$.\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then $f'(x)<0$.\n\nThe information about $f'(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & (- ∞, 1) & 1 & (1, 4) & 4 & (4, + ∞) \\\\ f & ↘ & min & ↗ & max & ↘ \\\\ f^{′} & - & 0 & + & 0 & -\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f'(x)>0$ on the interval $(1,4)$."] [17592186105491 :question/worked-solution "To find where $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, we need to solve the inequality $f'(x) < 0$.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= 3x^{2} + 2x - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n3x^{2} + 2x - 8 < 0\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n3(x + 2)\\left(x -\\dfrac 4 3\\right) < 0\n$$\n\nand the graph of\n\n$$\ny=3(x+2)\\left(x -\\dfrac 4 3\\right)\n$$\n\nhas roots at $x=-2$ and\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac 4 3\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1359/Source/Images/75d6da8209635228de08be5e9bd44737.png)\n\nThe graph is negative on the interval\n\n$$\n\\left(-2, \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\n$$\n\nbetween the roots, so we conclude that $f'(x) < 0$ on the interval\n\n$$\n\\left(-2, \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval\n\n$$\n\\left(-2, \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\n$$"] [17592186105494 :question/problem "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{\\ln{2x}}{x}$ (plotted below) is strictly increasing.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1359/Source/Images/39ade411a2a5987fa7b7c2350ac26caf.png)"] [17592186105494 :question/worked-solution "To find where $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we need to solve the inequality $f'(x) > 0$.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x)$ using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= ((\\frac{1}{x}) \\cdot x - \\ln (2x) \\cdot 1)/(x^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - \\ln 2x}{x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1-\\ln{2x}}{x^2} > 0\n$$\n\nThe denominator is always positive, so this reduces to\n\n$$\n1 - \\ln 2x > 0⟹\\ln 2x < 1\n$$\n\nSolving $\\ln{2x} = 1$ gives the critical point\n\n$$\n2x = e\\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad x = \\dfrac{1}{2}e\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that this critical point is a maximum. We also see that the slope of the tangent lines is positive to the left of the critical point and negative to the right of the critical point.\n\nWe summarize this information in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & (0, \\frac{1}{2}e) & \\frac{1}{2}e & (\\frac{1}{2}e, ∞) \\\\ f & ↗ & max & ↘ \\\\ f^{′} & + & 0 & -\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the interval\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\frac 1 2 e\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich can also be written as\n\n$$\n0 < x < \\dfrac 1 2 e\n$$"] [17592186105495 :question/problem "Consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1359/Source/Images/57462b1da853695603be9e97d89cb7a4.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements is true?\n\n1. $f'(2.5) < f'(1) < f'(-1)$\n2. $f'(-1) < f'(1) < f'(2.5)$\n3. $f'(1) < f'(-1) < f'(2.5)$"] [17592186105495 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that\n\n- the slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is positive, so $f'(-1) > 0$.\n- the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is zero, so $f'(1) = 0$.\n- the slope of the tangent at $x=2.5$ is negative, and so $f'(2.5) < 0$.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is correct."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186102328 17592186102329 17592186102330 17592186102331 17592186102332 17592186104238 17592186104239 17592186104240 17592186104241 17592186104242] "immutable_fields" [[17592186102327 :answer-field/correct] [17592186102327 :answer-field/id] [17592186102327 :answer-field/key] [17592186102327 :answer-field/type] [17592186104237 :answer-field/correct] [17592186104237 :answer-field/id] [17592186104237 :answer-field/key] [17592186104237 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
